1 NAME Georg /Ratzinger/
2 GIVN Georg
2 _MARNM Ratzinger
2 NICK Robert Waldhausen
2 NICK Gottfried Wolf
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 3 APR 1844
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Germany
1 DEAT
2 DATE 3 DEC 1899
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Germany
1 OCCU Famous German Politician
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georg_Ratzinger_(politician) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Georg Ratzinger,  April 3, 1844 in Rickering at Deggendorf – December 3, 1899 in Munich) was a German Catholic priest, political economist, social reformer, author and politician. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He saw the gospel and Catholic social teaching as a means of empowering the poor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ratzinger was a pupil at the gymnasium at Passau during the years 1855-63, studied theology at Munich, 1863–67, and was ordained priest in 1867. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1868 he received the degree of Doctor of Theology at Munich.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the following years he devoted himself partly to pastoral, partly to journalistic work.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1869 he was chaplain at Berchtesgaden; 1870-71 he was editor of the journal "Fränkisches Volksblatt" at Würzburg; 1872–74, chaplain at Landshut, then editor, until 1876, of the "Volksfreund", at Munich.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a member of the Parliament of Bavaria from 1875 to 1878 and of the German Reichstag from 1877 to 1878. During this period he belonged to the Centre Party. He combined the roles of priest and politician in a way which his gr
2 CONC andnephew, Cardinal Joseph Ratzinger, later Pope Benedict XVI, rejected.
2 CONT
2 CONT With exception of a pastorate of three years at Günzelhafen, 1885–1888, he lived for a number of years at Munich, where he devoted himself to journalism and research.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1893 Ratzinger was again elected to the Bavarian Landtag, where he was now a moderate adherent of the "Bayerischer Bauernbund" (Bavarian Peasant Union) party, his views of social politics having caused him in the meantime to se
2 CONC ver his connections with the Centre Party. In 1898 he was again elected a member of the Reichstag. He remained a member of both bodies until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a literary man Ratzinger deserves much credit for his scholarly work in political economy and in historical subjects. His chief works, distinguished by erudition, richness of thought, and animated exposition, are: "Geschichte d
2 CONC er Armenpflege" (prize essay, Freiburg, 1868, 2nd revised ed., 1884); "Die Volkswirtschaft in ihren sittlichen Grundlagen. Ethnischsociale Studien über Cultur und Civilisation (Freiburg, 1881; 2nd. completely revised ed., 1895).
2 CONT
2 CONT The later work work maintains the ethical principles of Christianity as the only sure basis of political economy and opposes the materialistic system of what is called the "classical political economy" of Adam Smith.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Forschungen zur bayerischen Geschichte" (Kempten, 1898); this contains a large number of studies on early Bavarian history and on the history of civilization, based on a series of unconnected treatises, which had first appeared i
2 CONC n the "Historisch-politische Blätter". Of his smaller works the following should be mentioned: "Das Concil und die deustche Wissenschaft" (anonymously issued at Mainz, 1872) appeared first in the "Katholik", 1872, I; "Die Erhaltun
2 CONC g des Bauernstandes" (Freiburg, 1883).
2 CONT
2 CONT His nephew was the police officer Joseph Ratzinger, Sr., father of Pope Benedict XVI (born Joseph Ratzinger) and Georg Ratzinger, the priest and church musician.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ "Georg Ratzinger". Catholic Encyclopedia. Georg Ratzinger (politician) in the German National Library catalogue (German) Georg Ratzinger alias Robert Waldhausen Jüdisches Erwerbsleben. Skizzen aus dem sozialen Leben der Gegenwar
2 CONC t (1892) Georg Ratzinger alias Gottfried Wolf Das Judentum in Bayern. Skizzen aus der Vergangenheit und Vorschläge für die Zukunft (1897)
2 CONT  This article incorporates text from a publication now in the public domain: Herbermann, Charles, ed (1913). Catholic Encyclopedia. Robert Appleton Company.
